{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed and documented in `ANSWER.md`. Here's the picture:\n\n## Why it was silent\n\nThe job was never broken as a scheduler — `launchctl print` shows `runs = 6`, `last exit code = 1`. It fired every night and failed every night, via two stacked bugs:\n\n1. **The agent call failed under launchd** — the `.err` log shows `Not logged in · Please run /login` on every run. The plist's `EnvironmentVariables` set only `PATH`, no `USER`, so the headless CLI couldn't reach its keychain login.\n2. **`status=$?` killed the script before it could report the failure** — in zsh `status` is read-only, so line 19 died with `read-only variable: status`, one line *before* the branch that sends the failure note. The messenger was downstream of the fatal line. That's why you got neither summaries nor failure notes.\n\nThe `.err` log had the answer all along — six identical pairs of the two error lines, one per nightly run since 2026-10-12.\n\n## What I changed\n\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — `status=$?` → `rc=$?`; prompt now piped via stdin (`print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p …`), which also removes the hangs-on-stdin-under-launchd risk; both notifications now include run duration (`SECONDS`), and the failure note points at both the `.md` output and the `.err` log, since the real error text lives in the latter.\n- `Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist` — added `USER=vk` to `EnvironmentVariables`.\n\n## Verification\n\n`zsh -n` and `plutil -lint` pass. I then rebuilt the launchd conditions in a scratch HOME (`env -i` with the plist's PATH plus HOME/USER) with fake `claude`/`notify.sh` — no Telegram calls made:\n\n- **Original script + failing agent** → exit 1, stderr reproduced the exact two lines from your real log, **0 notifications**. Bug confirmed.\n- **Fixed script + failing agent** → exit 1, failure notification **delivered** with exit code, duration, and both log paths.\n- **Fixed script + working agent** → exit 0, success notification delivered; the fake agent confirmed it received the prompt via stdin and the dated `.md` file was created fresh.\n\nOne thing I couldn't test from this snapshot: the real Telegram send and the actual keychain login — those need one real `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/com.vk.nightly-review` on your machine (after `bootout`/`bootstrap` to pick up the new plist), plus one deliberately forced failure to confirm the failure note arrives. Silence should now be impossible in both directions."}
